# scope.env — optional per-machine launcher settings.
#
# Copy to `scope.env` (git-ignored) and edit. Read by apps/scope-desktop/scope-control.js
# when the app boots from the desktop icon, which inherits no shell environment,
# and by the pi-scope launcher (pi-scope, pi-scope.cmd) when it is used as the
# entry point. Real environment variables always win, so
# `SCOPE_HOST=127.0.0.1 ./run.sh` still overrides whatever is set here.
# Plain KEY=VALUE lines; `#` comments allowed. On Windows the file path is
# apps\scope-desktop\scope.env — the same file, from the same directory.

# ── Reach Pi Scope from a phone on the same Wi-Fi ────────────────────────────
# Default is 0.0.0.0: Pi Scope binds every interface so the header's phone button
# shows a scannable QR with no setup. The QR opens the MOBILE layout (/m) — a
# sessions list, the live transcript, a composer, and a new-session sheet where
# you pick the workspace and model. Not the desktop screen shrunk down.
#
# Because that exposes the server to your network, only run on a network you
# trust: anything on the LAN that knows the token can drive your agents — the
# mobile layout runs prompts on this host, starts and deletes sessions, and
# retargets a project's team/mode — and adding &ui=desktop to the same URL gets
# the full UI, including the Terminal view, which is a real shell here. The token
# is minted randomly at boot and written to tmp/scope_token (mode 0600). The QR
# carries neither: it is only the address, and the phone asks for the one-time
# pairing code printed beside it, then trades that for the token (POST /pair). A
# photo of the QR therefore gets nobody in: the six-digit code dies after two
# minutes or as soon as one phone spends it, and the panel shows the next one.
#
# To keep this machine the only one that can connect, uncomment the next line:
# SCOPE_HOST=127.0.0.1

# ── Phone traffic is HTTPS (on by default) ───────────────────────────────────
# When Pi Scope is LAN-reachable it also listens for HTTPS on the next port up
# (SCOPE_PORT + 1, i.e. 43201 by default), and the phone panel and QR point
# there — so the pairing code, and the token the phone trades it for, are
# encrypted on the Wi-Fi instead of readable by anything else on the network.
# Only the phone uses that port: the desktop window, the launcher's health checks
# and stop.sh keep talking plain http:// to 127.0.0.1.
#
# The certificate is generated on first use and cached in ~/.pi-scope/tls. It is
# self-signed, because no public authority issues certificates for a 192.168.x.x
# address, so the phone shows "your connection is not private" once: tap
# Advanced → Proceed (Safari: Show Details → visit this website). That warning is
# expected; it does not mean the server is unreachable.
#
# Point these at a pair the phone already trusts (mkcert, an internal CA) to skip
# that step. Both must be set, and the certificate has to name the LAN address
# the QR uses:
# SCOPE_TLS_CERT=/path/to/cert.pem
# SCOPE_TLS_KEY=/path/to/key.pem
#
# Move the HTTPS listener, or turn it off and let phones use plain http://:
# SCOPE_TLS_PORT=43201
# SCOPE_TLS=0

# ── Pin the auth token (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# By default a random token is minted per run. Uncomment to keep the phone URL
# stable across restarts (e.g. if you bookmarked it). Generate a good one with:
#   openssl rand -hex 24        (macOS/Linux)
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(24).toString('hex'))"
# SCOPE_AUTH_TOKEN=

# ── Change the port (optional, default 43190) ───────────────────────────────
# Pin a different port here (or with SCOPE_PORT in the environment). You do not
# need it for a one-off second instance: if the port is already busy the desktop
# app pops up a chooser (open the running instance / kill the holder / start on
# another port), and starting on another port gives that instance its own token
# and database so the two never interfere.
# SCOPE_PORT=43190
